---
title: Moving from Resend
name: Moving from Resend
description: Move a Resend app to Postboi in steps, keeping Resend as the provider for as long as you like. Field mapping, webhooks, domains, and what doesn't carry over.
category: Getting started
---

You can move in three steps, and each one ships on its own. Stop after any of them.

1. **Swap the SDK, keep Resend.** Replace `resend.emails.send()` with `mail()` and set the
   provider to `resend`. Your mail still goes out through your Resend account, on your
   Resend plan, from your Resend domain. Nothing about delivery changes.
2. **Move sending to Postboi.** Add your domain to Postboi, then change one line of config.
   Your `mail()` calls stay the same.
3. **Retire Resend.** Point your webhooks at Postboi, move your lists, and take Resend's
   DNS records down once nothing sends through it.

If you're starting fresh rather than moving, [Quick start](/quick-start) is quicker.

## 1. Swap the SDK, keep Resend

Install Postboi and tell it to use Resend. `bunx postboi init` asks which provider you
send with: pick **bring your own provider**, then **Resend**, and it asks for your Resend
API key and writes the config. By hand it's one file:

```typescript
// postboi.config.ts
import { config } from 'postboi'

export default config({
	provider: 'resend',
	default: { from: 'Acme <hello@acme.com>' },
})
```

`RESEND_API_KEY` stays in your environment, where it already is.

Then change the sends. Before:

```typescript
import { Resend } from 'resend'

const resend = new Resend(process.env.RESEND_API_KEY)

const { data, error } = await resend.emails.send(
	{
		from: 'Acme <hello@acme.com>',
		to: ['ada@example.com'],
		subject: 'Your receipt',
		html: '<p>Thanks for your order.</p>',
		replyTo: 'support@acme.com',
		tags: [{ name: 'kind', value: 'receipt' }],
	},
	{ idempotencyKey: 'receipt/order-42' }
)
if (error) throw new Error(error.message)
```

After:

```typescript
import { mail } from 'postboi'

const { id } = await mail({
	to: 'ada@example.com',
	subject: 'Your receipt',
	body: '<p>Thanks for your order.</p>',
	reply_to: 'support@acme.com',
	tags: ['receipt'],
	idempotency_key: 'receipt/order-42',
})
```

`from` comes from the config default, and the plain-text part is written from `body` for
you, as Resend does from `html`.

**Errors throw.** Resend's Node SDK hands back `{ data, error }` and never throws. `mail()`
returns `{ id }` on success and throws a [`PostboiError`](/errors) on failure, with the
provider's own error name in `code`. If you checked `error` after every send, that becomes a
`try`/`catch`, or nothing at all where a failed send should fail the request anyway.

### Field by field

| Resend (Node SDK)                        | `mail()`                                   | Notes |
| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ | ----- |
| `from`                                   | `from`                                     | Or set it once as `default.from` in the config |
| `to`, `cc`, `bcc`                        | `to`, `cc`, `bcc`                          | A string or an array, as before |
| `replyTo`                                | `reply_to`                                 | |
| `subject`                                | `subject`                                  | |
| `html`                                   | `body`                                     | |
| `text`                                   | `text`                                     | Written from `body` when you leave it out |
| `react`                                  | `body: render(<Email />)`                  | See [React Email](#react-email) below |
| `headers`                                | `headers`                                  | |
| `attachments: [{ filename, content }]`   | `attachments: [new File([content], filename)]` | Standard `File` objects |
| `tags: [{ name, value }]`                | `tags: ['value']`                          | Plain strings, using only the letters, numbers, `_` and `-` Resend allows |
| `scheduledAt`                            | `scheduled_at`                             | A `Date`, an ISO 8601 string or `{ days: 1 }`, not `'in 1 hour'` |
| `{ idempotencyKey }` (second argument)   | `idempotency_key`                          | In the message itself |
| `resend.emails.cancel(id)`               | `cancel(id)`                               | `import { cancel } from 'postboi'` |
| `resend.batch.send([...])`               | `mail([...])`                              | One result per message, never throws. See [bulk sending](/bulk) |
| `template: { id, variables }`            | No equivalent                              | Resend-hosted templates live in Resend. Render to HTML in your code instead |
| `topicId`                                | No equivalent                              | Postboi's [lists](/provider#lists--broadcasts) handle opt-in |
| `attachments[].contentId` (inline `cid:` images) | Not carried over                   | Link images by URL instead |

A batch where everyone gets the same email with a few values swapped is shorter than
Resend's, and on Resend it still goes out as one request to its batch endpoint:

```typescript
await mail({
	to: ['ada@example.com', 'linus@example.com'],
	subject: 'Hey {name}',
	body: '<p>Hi {name}</p>',
	data: {
		'ada@example.com': { name: 'Ada' },
		'linus@example.com': { name: 'Linus' },
	},
})
```

### React Email

Resend takes a React element in `react`. Postboi takes HTML, so render it first with
React Email's own [`render()`](https://react.email/docs/utilities/render). The template
doesn't change:

```tsx
import { render } from '@react-email/render'
import { mail } from 'postboi'
import Welcome from './emails/welcome'

await mail({
	to: user.email,
	subject: 'Welcome to Acme',
	body: render(<Welcome name={user.name} />),
})
```

`body` takes the promise `render()` returns, so there's no need to await it.

## 2. Move sending to Postboi

Once your app sends through `mail()`, moving off Resend is configuration.

**Add your domain to Postboi.** Run `bunx postboi domains add acme.com`, or add it in the
dashboard, and publish the three DKIM CNAME records it shows you. They sit beside Resend's
records rather than replacing them: Resend signs with `resend._domainkey` and uses a `send.`
subdomain for bounces, while Postboi uses its own DKIM names and a `bounce.` subdomain. Both
can be live at once, so nothing you send through Resend is affected while you check the
domain. Your DMARC record stays as it is.

**Switch the provider.** Run `bunx postboi init` and pick **Postboi** to get a
`POSTBOI_TOKEN`, then change the one line:

```typescript
// postboi.config.ts
export default config({
	provider: 'postboi',
	default: { from: 'Acme <hello@acme.com>' },
})
```

That's the whole change to your app. Every send above works as it did, and you now get
[delivery status](/provider#delivery-status) per message, `mail.messages.get(id)`, a
message log and timeline in the dashboard, and `postboi sync`'s types, which reject a
`from` address your account can't send from at build time.

Two things to know first:

- **Limits are per plan.** The free plan sends 3,000 a month and 100 a day, the same as
  Resend's free plan. Above that you need a paid plan before you switch production over.
  See [limits](/provider#limits).
- **A new domain earns its reputation.** Mailbox providers judge a sending domain partly by
  its history. If you send in volume, move one kind of mail first (receipts, say) and the
  rest a few days later, rather than everything in one go.

Want to move gradually? Switch the provider per environment with `POSTBOI_PROVIDER`
([per-environment config](/manual-setup#per-environment-config)), or construct the two
providers side by side and pick per send while you compare:

```typescript
import Postboi from 'postboi/postboi'
import Resend from 'postboi/resend'

const postboi = new Postboi({ default: { from: 'hello@acme.com' } })
const resend = new Resend({ api_key: process.env.RESEND_API_KEY, default: { from: 'hello@acme.com' } })

const provider = kind === 'receipt' ? postboi : resend
await provider.send({ to, subject, body })
```

**Or keep Resend doing the delivery.** With your Resend key synced to Postboi, the
dashboard's **Send via** setting sends through your Resend account while Postboi keeps the
log, suppressions and timeline. Your code is the same as step 2. See
[relay](/provider#relay-send-via-your-own-provider) for what that does and doesn't report back.

## 3. Retire Resend

### Webhooks

[`postboi/webhooks`](/webhooks) reads Resend's webhooks and Postboi's into one event shape,
so you can rewrite your handler once and point either provider at it:

```typescript
import { webhook } from 'postboi/webhooks'

export const POST = webhook(async (event) => {
	if (event.type === 'bounced' && event.bounce?.category === 'hard') {
		await mark_undeliverable(event.email)
	}
})
```

While Resend is your provider, it verifies the signature with `RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET`, the
`whsec_…` from Resend's dashboard. Once you've switched, add the same URL as a webhook in
the Postboi dashboard and set `POSTBOI_WEBHOOK_SECRET` from there. The handler doesn't
change.

| Resend event             | `event.type` |
| ------------------------ | ------------ |
| `email.sent`             | `sent`       |
| `email.delivered`        | `delivered`  |
| `email.delivery_delayed` | `delayed`    |
| `email.bounced`          | `bounced`    |
| `email.complained`       | `complained` |
| `email.opened`           | `opened`     |
| `email.clicked`          | `clicked`    |
| `email.failed`           | `failed`     |

Resend's `email.scheduled`, `email.suppressed` and `email.received`, and its `contact.*`,
`domain.*` and `topic.*` events, aren't read. On Postboi, a reply arrives as a `received`
event (see [replies](/provider#replies)).

### Lists and contacts

Resend's audiences and contacts don't move automatically. Export them from Resend as CSV
and import the file into a list in the Postboi dashboard, where it goes through the same
double opt-in and suppression checks as every other way onto a list. Broadcasts on Postboi
are ordinary sends on your plan, with no separate charge per contact. See
[lists and broadcasts](/provider#lists--broadcasts).

### Suppressions

Addresses Resend has suppressed for hard bounces or complaints should stay suppressed. Add
them before your first send from Postboi with
[`mail.suppressions.add()`](/provider#suppressions).

### DNS

When nothing has sent through Resend for a while, remove Resend's `resend._domainkey` record
and its `send.` subdomain records. Leave Postboi's DKIM records and your DMARC record alone.

## What carries over and what doesn't

**Moves with your code:** recipients, subject, HTML and text, attachments, headers, tags,
scheduling and cancelling, idempotency keys, batches, and webhook handling. All of it is
the same `mail()` call on either provider.

**Stays in Resend:** hosted templates and their variables, topics, inline `cid:` images,
and Resend's audiences and broadcasts, which you export and re-create.

**Postboi doesn't have yet:** SDKs in languages other than TypeScript (anything else talks
to the [REST API](/rest-api)), an SMTP relay for sending into Postboi, and dedicated IPs.
If you depend on one of those, keep Resend as your provider and use step 1 alone. The
[comparison](/compare) has the rest.
